## Custom Audit Rules for CVE-2026-126714 phpaftershell IOC Detection

## ref: https://depthfirst.com/research/phpaftershell

## Detection rules for PHP session-file corruption / deserialization RCE where
## attacker-controlled code executes in an Apache httpd worker.
##
## Target platform: RHEL-family systems using:
##   - Apache service account: apache ## the rules below need to be adapted if another user account is configured
##   - Apache binary: /usr/sbin/httpd
##   - PHP session directory: /var/lib/php/session
##
## IMPORTANT
##
## 1. Confirm the effective PHP session path before deployment:
##      php -i | grep '^session.save_path'
##      grep -R '^[[:space:]]*session.save_path' /etc/php.ini /etc/php.d 2>/dev/null
##
## 2. If PHP uses /var/lib/php/sessions, /tmp, or another directory, replace
##    /var/lib/php/session/ in every applicable rule. Do not watch all of /tmp
##    on a busy server unless unavoidable.
##
## 3. These rules detect host activity and post-exploitation behavior. They do
##    not prove exploitation from one event. Correlate session writes/truncation,
##    worker death or OOM, reopening of the same sess_* file, Apache child
##    execution, and unusual outbound connections.
##
## 4. Test with augenrules --check before loading. Unsupported syscalls on older
##    kernels should be removed rather than allowing the complete ruleset to fail.
##
## 5. Do not enable immutable mode (-e 2) until testing is complete.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## PHP session directory changes
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Broad, low-complexity coverage of writes and attribute changes. The watch
## records the affected sess_* path and is useful even when syscall rules differ
## between kernel versions.
-w /var/lib/php/session/ -p wa -k php_session_change

## Record successful writes and truncations by the Apache effective identity.
## pwrite64 and ftruncate are particularly relevant to retained-tail session
## corruption. write/writev cover alternative PHP and filesystem code paths.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S write,writev,pwrite64,truncate,ftruncate -F euid=apache -F dir=/var/lib/php/session/ -F success=1 -k php_session_write
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S write,writev,pwrite64,truncate,ftruncate -F euid=apache -F dir=/var/lib/php/session/ -F success=1 -k php_session_write

## Record creation, opening, removal, and renaming of PHP session files.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S open,openat,creat,unlink,unlinkat,rename,renameat -F euid=apache -F dir=/var/lib/php/session/ -F success=1 -k php_session_lifecycle
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S open,openat,creat,unlink,unlinkat,rename,renameat -F euid=apache -F dir=/var/lib/php/session/ -F success=1 -k php_session_lifecycle

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Process execution from an Apache worker
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## On a conventional mod_php/httpd deployment, an Apache worker should not
## normally execute shells, interpreters, downloaders, or administration tools.
## Alert on every successful exec by the Apache effective identity, then enrich
## the event using exe=, comm=, proctitle=, cwd=, and PATH records.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve,execveat -F euid=apache -F success=1 -k apache_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve,execveat -F euid=apache -F success=1 -k apache_exec

## High-priority executables include:
##   /bin/sh, /bin/bash, /usr/bin/dash, /usr/bin/curl, /usr/bin/wget,
##   /usr/bin/nc, /usr/bin/ncat, /usr/bin/socat, /usr/bin/python3,
##   /usr/bin/perl, /usr/bin/php, /usr/bin/base64, /usr/bin/openssl,
##   /usr/bin/ssh, /usr/bin/chmod, /usr/bin/chown, and /usr/bin/systemctl.
## Keep the generic exec rules above: hard-coded tool lists are easily bypassed.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Outbound network activity from the Apache identity
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## connect() provides the strongest signal for reverse shells, callbacks, and
## payload retrieval. socket() adds context but may be noisy. Start with connect;
## enable the socket rules only if event volume is acceptable.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S connect -F euid=apache -F success=1 -k apache_network
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S connect -F euid=apache -F success=1 -k apache_network
# -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S socket -F euid=apache -F success=1 -k apache_socket
# -a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S socket -F euid=apache -F success=1 -k apache_socket

## auditd records the syscall and socket address data, but it is not a complete
## flow sensor. Correlate with nftables, firewalld, Suricata, Zeek, Falco,
## Tetragon, or the installed EDR for destination IP, port, DNS, and process tree.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Worker disruption and attempts to manipulate processes
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Signals sent by the Apache identity can indicate exploit orchestration or
## post-exploitation process control. Kernel/cgroup OOM kills do not necessarily
## originate from kill(), so also ingest the kernel journal.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S kill,tkill,tgkill -F euid=apache -k apache_signal
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S kill,tkill,tgkill -F euid=apache -k apache_signal

## Correlate with:
##   journalctl -k --grep='oom-kill|Out of memory|Memory cgroup out of memory|Killed process'
## Also monitor httpd worker exits, connection resets, worker respawning, and
## cgroup memory.current approaching memory.max.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Post-exploitation file and persistence activity
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## These rules are in addition to the base rules.d for LOTL detection.

-w /etc/php.ini -p wa -k php_config_change
-w /etc/php.d/ -p wa -k php_config_change

## Permission and ownership changes performed by the Apache identity.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S chmod,fchmod,fchmodat,chown,fchown,fchownat,lchown -F euid=apache -k apache_permission_change
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S chmod,fchmod,fchmodat,chown,fchown,fchownat,lchown -F euid=apache -k apache_permission_change

## Credential-changing syscalls from an Apache worker are critical.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S setuid,setgid,setreuid,setregid,setresuid,setresgid -F euid=apache -k apache_credential_change
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S setuid,setgid,setreuid,setregid,setresuid,setresgid -F euid=apache -k apache_credential_change

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Deployment and validation guidance
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Install:
##   install -o root -g root -m 0600 53-php-session-rce.rules \
##     /etc/audit/rules.d/53-php-session-rce.rules
##
## Validate and load:
##   augenrules --check
##   augenrules --load
##   auditctl -l
##   auditctl -s
##
## Confirm each key is producing events:
##   ausearch -k php_session_change -ts recent -i
##   ausearch -k php_session_write -ts recent -i
##   ausearch -k php_session_lifecycle -ts recent -i
##   ausearch -k apache_exec -ts recent -i
##   ausearch -k apache_network -ts recent -i
##   ausearch -k apache_signal -ts recent -i
##
## Monitor auditctl -s for lost events and backlog pressure. If lost is nonzero,
## the resulting telemetry is incomplete. Size the audit backlog and dispatcher
## configuration for measured peak load rather than relying on a fixed default.
##
## Suggested SIEM correlation window: 30 seconds on the same host/session.
## Raise HIGH when:
##   1. php_session_write records pwrite64 or ftruncate against sess_*, AND
##   2. httpd or php is killed, OOM-killed, or unexpectedly respawned, AND
##   3. the same session is reopened, followed by apache_exec, apache_network,
##      a write outside approved application data paths, or a credential change.
##
## Raise CRITICAL immediately for apache_exec involving a shell, interpreter,
## downloader, privilege tool, or unexpected binary, even if the preceding
## session-corruption sequence is unavailable.
##
## Complementary prevention:
##   - Apply the corrected PHP package.
##   - Disable session.upload_progress.enabled when it is not required.
##   - Use a dedicated, root-managed PHP session directory instead of /tmp.
##   - Restrict Apache egress with nftables/firewalld or a host firewall policy.
##   - Keep SELinux enforcing and investigate AVC denials rather than disabling it.
##
## Optional immutable mode, only after validation and change-control approval:
##   Add '-e 2' as the final rule in the complete audit policy, not necessarily
##   in this component file. Changing audit rules will then normally require a
##   reboot.
